45: ### The module and its interface
46: 
47: One new module, `replay-diary`. Its interface has three operations and nothing else.
48: 
49: | Operation | Takes | Gives back | Invariants |
50: |---|---|---|---|
51: | `admit(lesson)` | The receipt of a lesson that delivered a pulse: `stimulus` (the input: photoreceptor indices and currents), `teaching` (dopamine group and current), `lesson_index`, `receipt_hash` | Nothing | Unknown events are refused. Any entry with the same `stimulus_hash` is removed first (correction). Then Algorithm R decides whether the lesson is kept and which entry it replaces. No field value other than `stimulus_hash` affects any decision |
52: | `nextReplay(lesson_index)` | The index of the lesson just taught | One stored `(stimulus, teaching)`, chosen uniformly at random among entries from earlier lessons, or nothing if there are none | Never looks at the stimulus to choose. Never returns more than one entry. Never returns a score, count or average |
53: | `state()` / `restore(bytes)` | �?" / saved bytes | The diary's bytes and random-stream states / a diary | Saved in the same atomic write as the learner state and receipt |
54: 
55: **What the interface deliberately lacks.** There is no `query(input)`, no "find similar", no "what happened last time", and no score. Nothing can ask the diary about a board. That absence is the design: the only path out of the diary leads back into `teach`, the same lesson step the fly uses for real lessons, and the test runner never constructs a diary at all.
56: 
57: ### The data
58: 
59: | Field | Size | Meaning |
60: |---|---|---|
61: | `stimulus` | a few thousand (index, current) pairs, about 16 KiB | Exactly what the fly's photoreceptors received for the move, including the move highlight and the dim captured piece |
62: | `stimulus_hash` | 32 bytes | SHA-256 of `stimulus`; the experience's identity |
63: | `teaching` | a few bytes | Which dopamine cells were driven (reward: PAM07 and PAM02; punishment: PPL101 and PPL103) and the current (0.75 today) |
64: | `lesson_index` | 4 bytes | When the lesson happened |
65: | `receipt_hash` | 32 bytes | Links the entry to its receipt, so an auditor can rebuild the diary |
66: 
67: Capacity: one quarter of the stage's lessons (stage 2: 240 entries, about 4 MiB).
68: 
69: ### The practice loop
70: 
71: ```
72: for each lesson n:
73:     stimulus = Eyes(board, move)                     # move: the fly's own choice, or the tape's
74:     pulse    = checker(board, move)                # certificate goes to the audit log only
75:     if pulse is unknown: continue                  # no lesson, no entry, no replay
76:     teach(stimulus, pulse)                         # fly's rule changes its own connections
77:     diary.admit(receipt)
78:     save receipt + learner state + diary together  # one atomic write
79:     if this is a test point: test(copy of learner state)        # before the replay; no diary
80:     old = diary.nextReplay(n)
81:     if old: teach(old.stimulus, old.teaching); log dopamine and update sizes
82: ```
83: 
84: `teach` is the existing lesson step of `three-board-feedback-v1.mjs` (lines 85�?"103), taken out into one function so that real lessons and replays cannot differ: `h = forward(stimulus + pulse)`, dopamine signal `D` from the dopamine cells' rates, then each learnable gain `g ��? clip(g + ETA �� D �� (k + g �^' 1), 0.2, 1.8)`. Stage 1 uses the cubic-exit task's own lesson step in the same place.
