research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:167:Storing the delivered input instead of the board removes the leak the peer review found: two boards that differ only in invisible move counters produce identical eye input, so they are now the same experience. It also removes any need for chess code in the diary. An entry is about 16 KiB (a few thousand photoreceptor values), so even a diary holding every lesson of stage 2 (960 entries) needs about 15 MiB.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:173:1. Pick one entry uniformly at random among entries written before the current lesson and not superseded, using the replay random stream (section 4.8).
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:182:After every online lesson, exactly one replay [choice]. Reason: it interleaves old lessons at the finest grain and fixes the extra practice at one step per lesson, so controls can match it exactly. A trial whose verification was unknown delivered no pulse, so it is not a lesson: it gets no entry and no replay. The first lesson has no earlier entry and gets no replay; every replay arm skips the same step. All fly test snapshots, including the sawtooth checkpoints, are taken **after an online lesson and before its replay**, so the test sees the fly at the low point of any refresh cycle, not just after an old lesson was re-practised. Snapshots are scored by the test function, which receives a copy of the learner state and no diary.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:191:Before admitting a lesson, any entry with the same `stimulus_hash` is removed, whatever its pulse. Supersession depends only on whether the fly received exactly the same input before, never on the verdict. In chess this matters only in reversal tests, because a verified certificate gives the same verdict every time.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:202:The complete learner state is saved at every checkpoint: connection strengths, eligibility traces and any slow stores the learner keeps (stage 1's learner has a reservoir state), the diary, the reservoir counter and every random-stream state. One lesson's update, receipt and diary admission are published together in one atomic write (temporary file, flush, rename), with SHA-256 hashes in the checkpoint manifest, so a resume never has one without the others. The replay that follows is published in its own atomic write (its update, its log entry and the random-stream states) before the next lesson starts, so a resume restarts at a lesson boundary. Resume goes into a new directory after checking input hashes. Test and validation processes receive only the learner state, never the diary.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:206:Each run seed derives separate streams by SHA-256 of the seed and a label: `tape`, `choice`, `reservoir`, `replay`, `shuffle`, `filler`, `features`. One stream never feeds another's decisions, so, for example, admitting a lesson cannot change which entry is replayed next except through the diary's contents.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:226:2. **Some forgetting comes from block practice rather than from lessons that need incompatible connection settings** (hypothesis). The seed-4102 certificate shows only that resetting six shared connections to their resting values cannot hold both memories ([interference limits](../../../research/reports/interference-limits-2026-11-02/README.md)).
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:251:1. **Write rule:** the diary rebuilt from receipts equals the saved diary byte for byte; a planted certificate field, a validation stimulus or an unknown-event entry is rejected.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:275:| **Raw-feature learner with diary** | Simple learner (below) on raw photoreceptor input, same lessons, same diary and replay | Rival 6 |
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:286:**Contract.** Reuse the cubic-exit task's harness (`run-eye-graph-memory-cubic.mjs`; [specification](../../../research/reports/memory-retention-continuation-2026-11-01/cubic-exit-spec.md)) through an adapter. Six seeds, as the original. Four items learned in sequence, each a choice between two injected, normalized Kenyon-cell codes; one unknown trial in eight; then the delay with noise; then reversal of item 0. Teaching level is the task's prediction-scaled function (`TONIC + (pulse − TONIC) × scale`), recomputed at replay from the stored valence and the present prediction. Replay is inserted after each feedback trial in every phase that has feedback; the delay has none. The task's own arms (informative, absent, shuffled, disabled) are kept; the arms of section 7.2 except mixed order, case recall and the learners are added. Complete state includes the task's slow store. A stage 1 protocol file freezes the adapter, the exact per-seed formulas below and the seed list before any run.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:292:3. **No unrelated loss after reversal:** the reversed item chosen correctly with margin at least 0.15, every other item still chosen correctly, and their median margin ratio to the delay test at least 0.8 (third mark, per seed, with the added every-item condition).
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:293:4. **Kept over the delay:** every item correct after the delay and median margin ratio at least 0.7 (fourth mark, per seed).
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-5.md:398:2. Unknown-verification trials: stated that they are not lessons, so they get no entry and no replay.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-3.md:20:2. **When memory was inside the fly, new lessons damaged old ones.** In the cubic-exit run, reversing one association in seed 4102 made an unrelated old association wrong (margin 0.140130 to −0.020325), and per-seed retention ratios included 0.4203, 0.5941 and 0.6832 although the six-seed aggregate passed at 0.7199 ([report](../../../research/reports/memory-retention-continuation-2026-11-01/README.md)).
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-3.md:65:| `stimulus` | The exact input the fly received for the move, as delivered: for chess, the Eyes v2 photoreceptor stimulation list (index, current); for stage 1, the injected Kenyon-cell code | The fly received exactly this |
