research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\lead-recheck-round2.md:52:1. Cubic-exit run (`runs/memory-retention-2026-11-01/eye-graph-memory-cubic-v1-recovery1/records.jsonl`): all five original marks pass on six-seed medians (delay ratio 0.7199). Per seed, the delay ratios are 0.7566, 0.5941, 0.6832, 0.8118, 0.8714 and 0.4203. After the reversal, seeds 4102, 4105 and 4106 each lose one unrelated association; no seed loses an old item at the end of learning. Seed 4102's item 3 margin goes from 0.140130 to -0.020325, matching the design's quote. The run took 6,157 s at 299 MiB peak.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\lead-recheck-round2.md:54:3. Eyes v2 input size, graph-free, 83 moves on three boards: 1,644 active photoreceptors on average, at most 1,955, so 13-16 KiB per diary entry.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\lead-recheck-round2.md:62:- *Foundational (does a pass answer the question?):* stage 1 asked the diary to stop delay losses that occur with no lessons and so no replays, which is outside its mechanism; a working diary would have failed. Revision 6 scopes the delay as "not worse", and counts benefits only after new learning. The diary's link to reasoning is now stated: it keeps earlier ideas alive so the fly can combine them; the combining is the fly's.
