research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\README.md:3:Independent reviews of the [replay diary memory design](../../../documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md), a Volume 3 memory that re-plays the fly's own past lessons into its own learning rule and is removed before every test. Design only: nothing was built or run. Journal: [2026-11-17 entry](../../journal/experiments/2026-11-17-replay-diary-memory-design.md).
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-review-r2-foundational.prompt.md:29:- Revision 6: the lead's solo deep-in-thought pass. Most important: the four replay controls now change only replays of old lessons (entries from earlier blocks), so through the first block each is in exactly the diary arm's state and later differences measure keeping, not learning. Stage 1's delay condition is now "not worse than no diary", because raw records show delay losses happen when there are no lessons and so no replays; benefit conditions count only damage after new learning. Also: prerequisite requires no-diary acquisition, runtime corrected (about 22 hours single process), entry size measured, bootstrap seed and tie tolerance specified, development boards disjoint from the tape, early-stopped runs, a worked example, order-not-counterbalanced limit.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:57:One entry per teaching event the fly actually received during practice, written after the online lesson and its receipt are saved.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:65:| `image_hash` | SHA-256 of the photoreceptor input rendered at the original lesson | Lets replay prove the fly sees exactly what it saw |
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:74:1. Pick one entry at random (seeded), uniformly among entries written before the current online lesson that are not superseded (section 4.5).
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:84:After every online lesson, exactly one replay [choice]. Reason: it interleaves old lessons at the finest grain, as the complementary-learning-systems account requires, and it fixes the extra practice at exactly one step per lesson, so the matched controls can copy it exactly. A larger ratio is a separate, later question. The first lesson of a run has no earlier entry and gets no replay; every replay arm skips the same steps.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:89:- Eviction: reservoir sampling ([Vitter 1985](https://doi.org/10.1145/3147.3165)). When the diary is full, the n-th lesson replaces a random entry with probability capacity / n. Every past lesson then has the same chance of being kept, whatever its board, move or pulse. Variants that balance kept entries by class use outcome labels ([Chaudhry et al. 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10486)) and are not allowed.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:90:- Size: about 180 bytes per entry, so 4,096 entries take about 720 KiB, far inside the 2,048 MiB budget. Replay adds one forward pass per lesson, against roughly 30 forward passes to score all legal moves, so practice time rises by a few percent.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:94:If the fly receives a new lesson for exactly the same board and move as an older entry, the older entry is marked superseded and is never replayed again. This uses an exact match of two stored fields only, at writing time; it never compares different boards. In chess this happens only in a reversal test or a shuffled-feedback arm, because a verified certificate gives the same verdict every time.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:109:The diary, its eviction counter and its random-number state are saved inside every checkpoint, in the same atomic publication as the connection strengths they belong to (write a temporary file, flush, rename), with SHA-256 hashes in the checkpoint manifest. A lesson's entry and its connection update are published together, so a resume never has one without the other. Resume goes into a new directory, rebuilds inputs, checks their hashes and continues. Test and validation processes receive only the connection snapshot.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:129:2. **Forgetting comes from block practice, not from lessons that need incompatible connection settings** (hypothesis). The seed-4102 certificate shows that resetting the six shared connections to their resting values cannot hold both memories ([interference limits](../../../research/reports/interference-limits-2026-11-02/README.md)); it does not rule out other joint settings. The interleaved-from-start arm tests this.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:153:1. Write rule: the diary rebuilt from receipts equals the saved diary byte for byte; a planted certificate field, a validation-board hash or an unknown-event entry is rejected.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:155:3. Reservoir: over 100,000 synthetic lessons, kept-entry frequencies pass a chi-square test for uniformity at the 1% level; changing any field's value does not change which entries are kept.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:156:4. Supersession: an identical board and move with a new pulse supersedes the old entry and nothing else.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:173:| **Lookup on the diary** | At test, scores each move by the outcomes of its three nearest diary entries (inverse-distance average; keys: raw photoreceptors, Kenyon-cell activity, and random features; the best key sets the bar) | Rival 4: memory-only control |
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:174:| **Simple learner with the diary** | A linear reader on raw photoreceptors with a reward-signed update, the same lessons, the same diary and replay | Rival 5: simpler-system control |
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:184:**Task:** copy the cubic-exit comparison exactly: six seeds, four arbitrary associations, its delays, reversal, marks R1 to R5 and the 0.70 retained-margin ratio ([specification](../../../research/reports/memory-retention-continuation-2026-11-01/cubic-exit-spec.md); [report](../../../research/reports/memory-retention-continuation-2026-11-01/README.md); original runs `runs/memory-retention-2026-11-01/eye-graph-memory-cubic-v1` and its `-recovery1` continuation). In that run, reversing one association in seed 4102 made an unrelated old association wrong (margin 0.140130 to −0.020325). Change only the arms in section 7.2. The run protocol freezes the copied values before any run.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-revision-1.md:188:**Measures:** each association's ranking after acquisition, after delay and after reversal; retention ratio as originally defined; the number of runs that lose an unrelated association during reversal; reversal success; overwritten connections.
research/reports/replay-diary-design-review-2026-11-17\design-review-r1-skeptic.md:29:   A permutation preserves pulse counts **per stored entry**, not per replayed event. Two entries carrying reward and punishment, replayed nine times and once, deliver nine rewards originally but only one after swapping. Thus the shuffle can change reward/punishment balance despite preserving the entries and schedule.

